course: dictionary
lesson: 10007
title: Everyday words for TV
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Telly is a short, friendly word for television. People in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand use it all the time, especially when they chat. «What's on the telly?» means what programme is on television.
explain: Telly can mean two things. It can be the box in the room: «Mum switches the telly off» means she turns the television off. It can also mean the programmes: «What's on the telly?» asks which show is on.
explain: Context tells you which meaning is right. In «By eight o'clock Mum is asleep on the sofa with the telly still on», the telly is the set, and it is still switched on. The relaxed tone also tells you that telly is an informal, everyday word.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in. «They watch television till 10» still makes sense, so telly means television. In a formal letter you would write television, but in a chatty story telly sounds natural.
fact: Telly is an informal word for television, used mainly in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «Telly» can mean the television set itself or the programmes shown on it.
fact: Common phrases include watch telly, on the telly, turn the telly on, turn the telly off and telly addict.
fact: Informal words are fine in talk and stories, but in a formal essay you should write television.
fact: Telly is a noun, and its plural is tellies.
exercise: «Mum switches the telly off, sits down and pulls her in.» What does telly mean here? | *The television set | The bedtime story | The bedroom door | The sofa
exercise: «What's on the telly?» asks which of these? | *The programmes shown on television | A type of football | A brand of tea | The electricity bill
exercise: Which word is the everyday, informal word for television? | Screen | Monitor | Cinema | *Telly
test: In «Shouting at the telly or at a football match is common», who is the shouting NOT aimed at? | *The people at home | The television | The referee | The football players
test: Which phrase from the lesson means turn the television on? | *Turn the telly on | Watch telly | Telly addict
exam: Which sentence uses telly to mean the programmes, not the set? | *«What's on the telly tonight?» | «The telly is by the window.» | «She dusted the telly.» | «Mum switches the telly off.»
